  "refuter_findings": [
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:5:1",
      "id": "lem-riesz-lemma",
      "location": "Proof 1.1",
      "defect": "unlicensed-inference",
      "evidence": "The proof says: “If d=0, then for every k≥1 there is m_k∈M with ‖y-m_k‖<1/k, so m_k→y.” From the separate existential choices for each k it has formed an ℕ-indexed sequence in M; that implication uses countable choice and is not licensed here. Closedness instead directly gives a positive ball about y disjoint from M.",
      "severity": "nonfatal",
      "observed_sha256": "862f0dea0a2f13ef0d0eea2890248d9d2c73ed899beeafc0865e8e184f105bac"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:5:2",
      "id": "rem-discontinuous-functionals-on-banach-spaces-and-choice",
      "location": "Remark",
      "defect": "missing-hypothesis",
      "evidence": "The Remark says, without an infinite-dimensional hypothesis, “Full Choice gives one immediately from a Hamel basis: pick a basis, prescribe an unbounded coefficient functional on it, and extend linearly.” For the Banach space X=ℝ, every Hamel basis is finite and every linear functional x↦cx is continuous; no unbounded coefficient functional can be prescribed on its singleton basis. The claimed construction requires an infinite Hamel basis.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "2c13dc3ceba15469d5e73e1cbfeda418453068fc4c0c90fe00e696f8f42eb8c7"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:5:3",
      "id": "thm-banach-space-no-countably-infinite-hamel-basis",
      "location": "Statement",
      "defect": "false-claim",
      "evidence": "The Statement calls it equivalent that “there is no sequence (b_n) whose image is a basis of X.” Take the Banach space X=ℝ and b_n=1 for every n. Its image {1} is a Hamel basis, while it is finite rather than countably infinite. Accordingly, the proof's step 1.2 also cannot conclude every F_N is proper from its stated contradiction assumption.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "d27968ba9f9dfd61c7548c5bc7b5bb8c05e5bccdaef9eee7962316115830bae9"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:5:4",
      "id": "thm-banach-space-no-countably-infinite-hamel-basis",
      "location": "Proof 3.2 and 5.1",
      "defect": "unlicensed-inference",
      "evidence": "Step 3.2 repeatedly says “then choose 0<r_{n+1}<r_n/2” and concludes “Thus one obtains nested closed balls,” but supplies no canonical rule for choosing these real radii. L5 only supplies least elements of nonempty subsets of ℕ and makes the point choices canonical; it does not select the r_n. Producing the asserted infinite nested-ball sequence therefore still spends dependent/countable choice, contrary to step 5.1's claim that the written proof used only the fixed enumeration d.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "d27968ba9f9dfd61c7548c5bc7b5bb8c05e5bccdaef9eee7962316115830bae9"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:5:5",
      "id": "thm-kuratowski-wojdyslawski-embedding",
      "location": "Proof 6.1",
      "defect": "unlicensed-inference",
      "evidence": "The main Statement assumes “(M,d) be a bounded nonempty metric space,” but step 6.1 applies steps 1.1–5.1 to every metrizable (X,T). The metrizable-space definition permits X=∅, for which no basepoint and hence no K_o exists. The final conclusion is true for ∅ by a separate trivial embedding, but that case is not supplied.",
      "severity": "nonfatal",
      "observed_sha256": "72582b9edf62e1e09395bb164cb4ab63fbe1ff6714e429319e4d1369a3ae2002"
    }
  ],
